WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.201 --> 00:00:02.434
-Our next guest
is a comedian and writer

00:00:02.434 --> 00:00:04.770
whose book, "Jews Don't Count,"
is available now.

00:00:04.770 --> 00:00:06.706
Please welcome to the show
David Baddiel.

00:00:06.706 --> 00:00:08.708
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:08.708 --> 00:00:18.717 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:18.717 --> 00:00:20.486
-How are you?
-I'm all right.

00:00:20.486 --> 00:00:23.222
Actually, I'm jet-lagged
'cause I flew in from London,

00:00:23.222 --> 00:00:24.890
so I was up at 4:00 a.m.
this morning.

00:00:24.890 --> 00:00:27.326
But it's okay
'cause I'm an insomniac.

00:00:27.326 --> 00:00:30.429
-Oh, okay. So, yeah, kind of
those dovetail together nicely.

00:00:30.429 --> 00:00:32.665
-Yeah, but the thing is --
You didn't do it,

00:00:32.665 --> 00:00:34.100
but when you tell people
you're an insomniac,

00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:35.568
I've noticed, they often say,

00:00:35.568 --> 00:00:38.371
"Oh, I'm always asleep as soon
as my head hits the pillow."

00:00:38.371 --> 00:00:39.905
And I always want to say,
"Right, if I told you

00:00:39.905 --> 00:00:41.307
I was blind, would you have
said, 'Oh, really?

00:00:41.307 --> 00:00:43.276
I can see perfectly'"?
[ Laughter ]

00:00:43.276 --> 00:00:44.977
-You're right. It's very unfair
that they do that.

00:00:44.977 --> 00:00:47.546
-It really is, yeah.
-I'm so glad now, looking back,

00:00:47.546 --> 00:00:49.081
that I didn't say that.
-Yeah, I'm glad you didn't.

00:00:49.081 --> 00:00:51.884
-'Cause it's true, David.
As soon as it hits the pillow...

00:00:51.884 --> 00:00:54.186
[ Laughter ]
So I know you to be --

00:00:54.186 --> 00:00:55.255
You're a comedian,
you're a writer,

00:00:55.255 --> 00:00:56.489
you've written screenplays.

00:00:56.489 --> 00:00:58.491
I did not realize this
about you.

00:00:58.491 --> 00:01:00.994
You wrote a football anthem.

00:01:00.994 --> 00:01:02.661
-Thanks for calling it football.

00:01:02.661 --> 00:01:04.964
-Well, I know
who I'm talking to, right?

00:01:04.964 --> 00:01:06.298
-I thought
you were gonna say soccer,

00:01:06.298 --> 00:01:09.102
and I was gonna have to claim
"No, we got there first."

00:01:09.102 --> 00:01:11.237
Yeah, no, that is one of
the things I am

00:01:11.237 --> 00:01:16.875
very well-known for in England,
which is that, in 1996,

00:01:16.875 --> 00:01:19.812
me and another comedian
and a band, The Lightning Seeds,

00:01:19.812 --> 00:01:21.047
we wrote a song called

00:01:21.047 --> 00:01:23.849
"Three Lions
(Football's Coming Home),"

00:01:23.849 --> 00:01:27.653
and it has gone to number one
in England four times.

00:01:27.653 --> 00:01:30.756
It holds the record for going
to number one four times.

00:01:30.756 --> 00:01:34.761
And the problem with that is,
that my voice is [bleep]

00:01:34.761 --> 00:01:36.495
That's what my voice is.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:36.495 --> 00:01:38.297
-And, like, George Michael
and Freddie Mercury

00:01:38.297 --> 00:01:40.633
must be turning in their graves
at the thought of it.

00:01:40.633 --> 00:01:42.935
It got -- When it came out --
I mean, it is really popular.

00:01:42.935 --> 00:01:44.737
Right?
It gets sung at the grounds.

00:01:44.737 --> 00:01:47.673
The Queen goes on TV and says
"Football's coming home,"

00:01:47.673 --> 00:01:49.475
and some --
it's really, really popular.

00:01:49.475 --> 00:01:52.678
But it got, like,
the worst review anything's,

00:01:52.678 --> 00:01:55.615
I think, ever got when it first
came out from "Q" magazine.

00:01:55.615 --> 00:01:56.949
-Yeah, sure,
I remember "Q" magazine.

00:01:56.949 --> 00:01:58.617
-Okay.
So "Q" magazine said --

00:01:58.617 --> 00:02:01.220
It started off well.
It said, "In the future,

00:02:01.220 --> 00:02:02.654
people will think of
'Three Lions'

00:02:02.654 --> 00:02:04.223
as better than it was.

00:02:04.223 --> 00:02:07.327
In the future," they said,
"folk memory will erase

00:02:07.327 --> 00:02:11.531
the memory of David Baddiel's
singing just as effectively

00:02:11.531 --> 00:02:12.999
as it has erased the memory

00:02:12.999 --> 00:02:15.134
of corpse robbers
during the Blitz."

00:02:15.134 --> 00:02:16.969
[ Laughter ]

00:02:16.969 --> 00:02:19.472
I'm, like, almost proud to be
insulted on such a scale.

00:02:19.472 --> 00:02:21.407
-It really is.
But just to give people a sense,

00:02:21.407 --> 00:02:24.410
this is a song you wrote being
sung at football grounds.

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:26.079
-Yeah.
-Let's just take a listen.

00:02:26.079 --> 00:02:29.682 line:0%
♪ It's coming,
football's coming home ♪

00:02:29.682 --> 00:02:33.353 line:0%
♪ It's coming home,
it's coming home ♪

00:02:33.353 --> 00:02:37.190 line:0%
♪ It's coming,
football's coming home ♪

00:02:37.190 --> 00:02:38.924 line:0%
-There are more lyrics
than what we showed there.

00:02:38.924 --> 00:02:40.126
-Yeah, there are.

00:02:40.126 --> 00:02:42.494
-I feel that doesn't show
the breadth of your work.

00:02:42.494 --> 00:02:43.763
-You're a sports fan, right?
-I am, yeah.

00:02:43.763 --> 00:02:45.365
-Okay. So, the main lyrics
are about how England

00:02:45.365 --> 00:02:47.900
are gonna lose, probably,
because that is actually

00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:49.869
the history of England
at big tournaments,

00:02:49.869 --> 00:02:51.671
but we think somehow
they're gonna win.

00:02:51.671 --> 00:02:54.106
And that's what chimed, I think,
with football fans.

00:02:54.106 --> 00:02:56.775
But I tell you something,
when it first got sung like that

00:02:56.775 --> 00:02:59.411
at Wembley Stadium,
it was, like,

00:02:59.411 --> 00:03:00.479
I didn't know
it was gonna happen.

00:03:00.479 --> 00:03:01.814
I'm sitting there
with Frank Skinner,

00:03:01.814 --> 00:03:04.650
who also wrote it, and,
suddenly, we've beaten Scotland,

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:07.020
and the whole stadium erupts.

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:09.021
And everyone knows the words,
right?

00:03:09.021 --> 00:03:11.324
-Yeah, you made it pretty easy.
-Well, yeah.

00:03:11.324 --> 00:03:14.394
[ Laughter ]

00:03:14.394 --> 00:03:15.962
Yeah, I did.

00:03:15.962 --> 00:03:18.064
I knew what I was playing with
these football fans, right?

00:03:18.064 --> 00:03:20.066
But that was amazing.
It's sort of --

00:03:20.066 --> 00:03:21.967
You know you're meant to say
that the best day of your life

00:03:21.967 --> 00:03:23.169
is when your kids are born.
-Yeah.

00:03:23.169 --> 00:03:24.236
-Yeah, [bleep] that.

00:03:24.236 --> 00:03:25.438
That was the best day
of my life.

00:03:25.438 --> 00:03:26.572
[ Laughter ]
-I believe it.

00:03:26.572 --> 00:03:28.240
-It was really amazing, yeah.

00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:29.875
-I want to talk about
what's inside the book,

00:03:29.875 --> 00:03:31.110
but let's start with the title.

00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:33.813
It's very provocative,
to say the least.

00:03:33.813 --> 00:03:36.315
It was very strange to say it
to the audience tonight.

00:03:36.315 --> 00:03:38.885
-Okay.
I noticed you qualified it

00:03:38.885 --> 00:03:41.053
by saying,
"He argues that they do."

00:03:41.053 --> 00:03:43.923
-Yeah, or they should, I guess,
is really what you argue.

00:03:43.923 --> 00:03:45.991
-Well, the title is provocative,
and a few people have --

00:03:45.991 --> 00:03:47.659
and, like, a woman wrote to me
from here, from New York,

00:03:47.659 --> 00:03:49.929
saying, "I've tried to read this
in the hair salon,

00:03:49.929 --> 00:03:52.532
and I got some weird looks."
Right?

00:03:52.532 --> 00:03:54.733
But in Germany -- so, this
book's come out in Germany,

00:03:54.733 --> 00:03:56.803
which perhaps shows
what the problem is.

00:03:56.803 --> 00:04:00.706
[ Laughter ]

00:04:00.706 --> 00:04:02.575
It's come out in Germany,
and the German publishers

00:04:02.575 --> 00:04:04.109
wrote to me,
and they actually said,

00:04:04.109 --> 00:04:06.578
"We can't call it --"
It's "Juden zahlen nicht" --

00:04:06.578 --> 00:04:07.847
"Jews Don't Count," in Germany.

00:04:07.847 --> 00:04:09.315
They wrote me
this very serious e-mail.

00:04:09.315 --> 00:04:10.917
They said, "With our history."

00:04:10.917 --> 00:04:13.419
And I was tempted to troll them
and say, "Sorry. What history?"

00:04:13.419 --> 00:04:14.920
-Yeah, exactly.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:14.920 --> 00:04:16.022
-"What are you talking about?"

00:04:16.022 --> 00:04:17.290
See how long I could keep
that going for.

00:04:17.290 --> 00:04:18.624
-Right, right, right.
-But eventually they said,

00:04:18.624 --> 00:04:20.092
"No, we can't do it," and they
called it something else.

00:04:20.092 --> 00:04:23.162
You've got it there.
They called it "Und die Juden?"

00:04:23.162 --> 00:04:26.465
question Mark, which means,
"And The Jews?", question mark,

00:04:26.465 --> 00:04:28.701
which sounds creepier to me.
-Yeah!

00:04:28.701 --> 00:04:32.071
-It sounds more like something
that an S.S. general would say

00:04:32.071 --> 00:04:34.540
to, like, Hitler.
Like, that's the last thing

00:04:34.540 --> 00:04:36.375
they say in the meeting,
and he just looks really cross.

00:04:36.375 --> 00:04:38.143
-Yeah.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:04:38.143 --> 00:04:42.882
-This is a book where you argue
that anti-Semitism

00:04:42.882 --> 00:04:45.718
should be judged
the same as racism,

00:04:45.718 --> 00:04:48.888
that it is, in fact, racism, and
it's often not valued the same.

00:04:48.888 --> 00:04:50.623
-Well, it's a book about
the anti-race --

00:04:50.623 --> 00:04:51.857
the identity politics
conversation.

00:04:51.857 --> 00:04:53.158
-Yeah.
-And about how,

00:04:53.158 --> 00:04:55.227
in that conversation,
which has intensified incredibly

00:04:55.227 --> 00:04:57.830
over the last 20 years, it seems
to me that anti-Semitism,

00:04:57.830 --> 00:05:00.799
which is a very old racism,
very old form of discrimination,

00:05:00.799 --> 00:05:03.235
is kind of very low
in the mix of that.

00:05:03.235 --> 00:05:05.671
It kind of feels like people
aren't so bothered about it.

00:05:05.671 --> 00:05:07.940
I start the book with about
12 examples of this.

00:05:07.940 --> 00:05:09.776
For example,
I'm sitting at home,

00:05:09.776 --> 00:05:13.846
I'm listening to Jeremy Irons
in 2019 read out

00:05:13.846 --> 00:05:16.449
all of T.S. Eliot's poetry
on BBC Radio 4,

00:05:16.449 --> 00:05:18.083
which is a very kind of
posh thing.

00:05:18.083 --> 00:05:19.785
And I know that I'm gonna get
to lunchtime,

00:05:19.785 --> 00:05:21.454
and he's gonna read out
the lines, which are real lines

00:05:21.454 --> 00:05:24.389
from T.S. Eliot, "The rats
are underneath the piles,

00:05:24.389 --> 00:05:26.358
the Jew is underneath the lot."

00:05:26.358 --> 00:05:28.260
And my point is not that
T.S. Eliot is an anti-Semite.

00:05:28.260 --> 00:05:30.730
We know it. The point is,
where's the reaction to that?

00:05:30.730 --> 00:05:33.766
In a trigger space that we exist
in, from microaggression

00:05:33.766 --> 00:05:36.869
against all minorities,
why is Twitter not alive

00:05:36.869 --> 00:05:39.438
with fury at that and statues
of T.S. Eliot being toppled?

00:05:39.438 --> 00:05:41.374
I'm not asking
for that to happen.

00:05:41.374 --> 00:05:44.243
I'm asking why there is no
conversation about it.

00:05:44.243 --> 00:05:47.180
-And why -- it must have
occurred to you

00:05:47.180 --> 00:05:49.048
and your publishers, as well,
coming at it

00:05:49.048 --> 00:05:52.117
from a comedian's perspective,
it doesn't seem like necessarily

00:05:52.117 --> 00:05:55.154
a topic that would be rich for,

00:05:55.154 --> 00:05:57.990
you know, sort of
comic mining of the material.

00:05:57.990 --> 00:06:00.326
What made you decide to want to
approach it in the first place?

00:06:00.326 --> 00:06:03.028
-Well, I don't just do comedy.
I've become --

00:06:03.028 --> 00:06:04.764
As I get older --
I find, as I get older,

00:06:04.764 --> 00:06:06.765
I'm allowed to talk about
serious stuff.

00:06:06.765 --> 00:06:08.300
-Yeah.
-There is jokes in it.

00:06:08.300 --> 00:06:11.337
I talk about, in fact, meeting
a Jewish football commentator

00:06:11.337 --> 00:06:13.839
who interviewed me once and told
me once he wasn't Jewish.

00:06:13.839 --> 00:06:15.708
And then I met him later on
at the World Cup in Japan

00:06:15.708 --> 00:06:18.544
in a sauna, and he was.
-Okay, yeah, yeah.

00:06:18.544 --> 00:06:20.512
[ Laughter ]
-So there are jokes in it.

00:06:20.512 --> 00:06:23.082
Right? Right?
But, yeah, it's mainly serious.

00:06:23.082 --> 00:06:24.917
I mean, the point
you made about racism,

00:06:24.917 --> 00:06:26.953
I note, in America,
this is a more complex thing,

00:06:26.953 --> 00:06:28.153
is that there seems
to be a problem with calling

00:06:28.153 --> 00:06:29.488
anti-Semitism racism.

00:06:29.488 --> 00:06:32.191
And if you want the argument
for it, I'm an atheist.

00:06:32.191 --> 00:06:34.994
But that would get me no
free passes out of Auschwitz.

00:06:34.994 --> 00:06:36.762
I've noticed
that white supremacists,

00:06:36.762 --> 00:06:39.465
they don't check whether
I keep kosher before they think,

00:06:39.465 --> 00:06:40.833
"I'll burn down
that Jew's house."

00:06:40.833 --> 00:06:42.001
Do you know what I mean?
-Sure.

00:06:42.001 --> 00:06:44.469
-Right? So, for me,
it's an accident of birth

00:06:44.469 --> 00:06:48.641
that the racists hate me for,
and so, therefore, it's racism.

00:06:48.641 --> 00:06:50.909
And, you know, I mean,
as it happens,

00:06:50.909 --> 00:06:53.279
being an atheist and a Jew
is kind of a weird thing

00:06:53.279 --> 00:06:54.580
because people
don't understand that, either.

00:06:54.580 --> 00:06:55.881
But, basically,
I don't believe in God,

00:06:55.881 --> 00:06:57.015
I believe in Larry David.

00:06:57.015 --> 00:06:59.485
It's quite straightforward
for me, right?

00:06:59.485 --> 00:07:00.719
Actually, can I just
tell you a quick story?

00:07:00.719 --> 00:07:03.022
-Please.
-Because I use that sometimes

00:07:03.022 --> 00:07:04.523
to get out of --
Because it's very common

00:07:04.523 --> 00:07:06.192
being an atheist and a Jew,
but I use it sometimes

00:07:06.192 --> 00:07:07.893
to get -- because
I'm well-known in Britain --

00:07:07.893 --> 00:07:09.395
to get out of stuff
I get asked to do

00:07:09.395 --> 00:07:11.030
by the local Jewish community.

00:07:11.030 --> 00:07:13.432
So this rabbi rang me up.
My local rabbi --

00:07:13.432 --> 00:07:15.267
I've never met him --
somehow got my number --

00:07:15.267 --> 00:07:16.669
and says,
"Can you light the menorah,

00:07:16.669 --> 00:07:18.637
the big menorah, in London?

00:07:18.637 --> 00:07:21.440
You come and light it, because
you're kind of Mr. Jew, right?"

00:07:21.440 --> 00:07:23.576
[ Laughter ]
And I didn't want to do it.

00:07:23.576 --> 00:07:25.311
So I played what I thought --
my trump card.

00:07:25.311 --> 00:07:26.445
I said,
"I'm sorry to tell you this,

00:07:26.445 --> 00:07:28.314
rabbi, but I'm an atheist."

00:07:28.314 --> 00:07:29.848
He said, "So am I."
I thought "[Bleep]

00:07:29.848 --> 00:07:31.950
I mean, like --"
[ Laughter ]

00:07:31.950 --> 00:07:35.921
How common has that become
amongst Jews, right?

00:07:35.921 --> 00:07:38.190
-I know that when you write
about something

00:07:38.190 --> 00:07:40.592
that is this provocative
in topic,

00:07:40.592 --> 00:07:43.495
you're gonna hear from,
you know, both sides.

00:07:43.495 --> 00:07:47.032
But I heard that you have
an Internet troll of a sort

00:07:47.032 --> 00:07:49.835
living in your own home.
-I do have that.

00:07:49.835 --> 00:07:52.805
I have my son Ezra, who --

00:07:52.805 --> 00:07:54.140
I don't know if
you've got a teenage son.

00:07:54.140 --> 00:07:55.942
-No, not yet.
-But it is like living

00:07:55.942 --> 00:07:57.242
with a, yeah, an Internet troll.

00:07:57.242 --> 00:07:59.344
He likes to humiliate me,
and he uses, I guess,

00:07:59.344 --> 00:08:02.081
my public profile against me,
to some extent.

00:08:02.081 --> 00:08:04.182
But he's clever,
and he's good with technology.

00:08:04.182 --> 00:08:07.420
So, for example,
we were in Cornwall last summer,

00:08:07.420 --> 00:08:10.122
and it's by the sea.
And I was just driving there

00:08:10.122 --> 00:08:13.692
with my family, and that summer,
my car stereo had packed up.

00:08:13.692 --> 00:08:17.829
So I was using a -- like,
a Bluetooth personal speaker

00:08:17.829 --> 00:08:19.164
for the music in the car.

00:08:19.164 --> 00:08:20.533
And it's just on the dashboard.

00:08:20.533 --> 00:08:23.369
We get out of the car.
It's a really crowded car park.

00:08:23.369 --> 00:08:24.936
I'm holding that in front of me
because I think,

00:08:24.936 --> 00:08:26.872
"I don't want it to be stolen."
I'm holding that.

00:08:26.872 --> 00:08:29.008
I'm holding, like, swimming
costumes and towels or whatever.

00:08:29.008 --> 00:08:31.376
It was really awkward.
I noticed that Ezra

00:08:31.376 --> 00:08:32.978
is doing something
with his phone,

00:08:32.978 --> 00:08:34.447
but I'm not really
paying attention.

00:08:34.447 --> 00:08:36.215
Then suddenly,
this speaker starts playing,

00:08:36.215 --> 00:08:39.785
incredibly loudly,
this incredibly Jewish music.

00:08:39.785 --> 00:08:41.186
Right?
[ Laughter ]

00:08:41.186 --> 00:08:44.457
[ Vocalizing to the tune of
"Hava Nagila" ]

00:08:44.457 --> 00:08:47.393
And I could see all the people
in the car park look around

00:08:47.393 --> 00:08:50.463
and clearly think, "Oh, look,
there's David Baddiel."

00:08:50.463 --> 00:08:52.030
[ Laughter ]

00:08:52.030 --> 00:08:53.966
"I guess he must
have to have music like that

00:08:53.966 --> 00:08:55.167
around him all the time.

00:08:55.167 --> 00:08:57.169
Any minute now,
he'll be doing this, I imagine."

00:08:57.169 --> 00:08:58.638
[ Laughter ]

00:08:58.638 --> 00:09:01.573
-Got to love a teenage kid
who can take it out of his dad.

00:09:01.573 --> 00:09:02.975
-You know what?
I was humiliated,

00:09:02.975 --> 00:09:04.743
but I was proud
because that is a good gag.

00:09:04.743 --> 00:09:05.977
-Yeah, exactly.
-Yeah.

00:09:05.977 --> 00:09:08.381
-It feels like he ticks
both the boxes of Judaism

00:09:08.381 --> 00:09:10.082
and comedy in the same moment.
-Exactly.

00:09:10.082 --> 00:09:11.383
That's what
I would want for him.

00:09:11.383 --> 00:09:12.651
-Thank you so much
for being here.

00:09:12.651 --> 00:09:14.019
Congratulations on the book.
-It's lovely to be here.

00:09:14.019 --> 00:09:15.621
-That's David Baddiel,
everybody.

00:09:15.621 --> 00:09:18.224
"Jews Don't Count" is on sale
now wherever books are sold,

00:09:18.224 --> 00:09:20.192
but please support local
and independent bookstores.

